mod('datastats','Data, graphs & statistics',
 'Two bars of different heights do not prove two groups differ. How spread out the data are, and how many measurements there are, decide that.',
 ['A <b>mean</b> summarizes a sample, but samples vary. The <b>standard deviation</b> (SD) says how far individual measurements typically fall from the mean. The <b>standard error of the mean</b> (SEM = SD/√n) says how precisely the mean itself is known, and it shrinks as you take more measurements.',
  'Error bars are a way to see this. If you plot the mean ± 2 SEM (about a 95% confidence interval) for two groups and the bars <b>do not overlap</b>, the difference is probably real. If they overlap a lot, the data do not support a difference. Always say which kind of error bar a graph uses.'],
 ('a bigger bar meant a bigger effect','how much the data scatter, or how many trials were run','a mean is only an estimate and carries uncertainty','SD, SEM and error bars to decide whether a difference is meaningful'),
 [('mean = Σx / n','average of the sample'),('SD = √[ Σ(x − mean)² / (n − 1) ]','spread of individual values'),('SEM = SD / √n','precision of the mean'),('95% CI ≈ mean ± 2 SEM','used for error bars')],
 [lab('ds-lab','Two groups with error bars',canvas='ds-canvas',aria='Bar graph of two group means with error bars. Controls change the means, spread and sample size.',
   seg=('ds-kind','Error bars show',[('sd','SD'),('sem','SEM'),('ci','2 × SEM (≈95% CI)')]),
   ctl=[('ds-ma','Mean of group A',10,40,1,20),('ds-mb','Mean of group B',10,40,1,26),('ds-sd','SD (both groups)',1,12,.5,6),('ds-n','Sample size n (both)',3,100,1,10)],
   out=[('ds-sem','SEM','x'),('ds-diff','difference in means','v'),('ds-ov','2 × SEM bars','')],own=True)],
 [('Leave the means at 20 and 26 with SD 6 and n 10. Do the 2 × SEM bars overlap? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#ds-kind [data-k=ci]"],["#ds-sd",6],["#ds-n",10]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','They are separated: SEM = 6/√10 ≈ 1.9, so each bar is ±3.8, and 20 + 3.8 < 26 − 3.8. The difference is likely real.'),
  ('Raise the SD to 12 without changing anything else. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ds-sd",12]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The bars now overlap heavily. More scatter means less confidence that the means differ, even though the means are the same.'),
  ('With SD 12, raise n to 100. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ds-sd",12],["#ds-n",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','SEM falls to 1.2, so the bars separate again. More measurements give a more precise mean.')],
 ('Plant heights (cm) after two treatments: A = 12, 14, 15, 11, 13; B = 17, 19, 16, 18, 20. Find the mean and SD of A and decide whether the groups likely differ.',[
  '<b>Mean of A.</b> (12+14+15+11+13)/5 = 13.',
  '<b>Deviations squared.</b> (−1)², 1², 2², (−2)², 0² = 1, 1, 4, 4, 0, sum 10.',
  '<b>SD.</b> √(10/(5−1)) = √2.5 ≈ 1.58. SEM = 1.58/√5 ≈ 0.71.',
  '<b>Compare.</b> B has mean 18 and the same SD, so 2 × SEM is about 1.4 for each group. 13 + 1.4 is far below 18 − 1.4, so the bars do not overlap: the treatments likely differ.']),
 ['Using n instead of n − 1 for a sample SD.','Confusing SD with SEM. SD describes the data; SEM describes the mean.','Saying overlapping error bars prove no difference. They mean the data do not show one.','Comparing bar heights without looking at the error bars or the sample size.','Forgetting to label the axes with units, or to say what the error bars represent.'],
 ('What a graph must show',['<b>Independent variable on the x-axis, dependent on the y-axis</b>, both labeled with units, with an even scale. Use a line graph for continuous x (temperature, time), a bar graph for categories. For each point or bar, plot a mean and error bars when there are replicates. In an FRQ, describe the trend with numbers ("rate doubled from 20 to 30 °C"), then explain the biology.']),
 [('Two group means have error bars of ±2 SEM that do not overlap. The best conclusion is',['the difference is likely real','the difference is certainly caused by the treatment','the sample size must be too small','the SDs must be equal'],0,'Non-overlapping 2 × SEM bars suggest the means differ, though design and controls still decide about cause.')])

mod('chisq','Chi-square test',
 'Flip a coin 100 times and you will not get exactly 50 heads. The chi-square test asks whether a miss is small enough to be luck.',
 ['Genetics predicts ratios, such as 3:1. Real counts never match exactly, so we need a rule for when the miss is too big. The <b>chi-square</b> statistic adds up how far each observed count is from the expected count, scaled by the expected count: χ² = Σ (o − e)² / e.',
  'Compare χ² with a <b>critical value</b> that depends on the <b>degrees of freedom</b> (df = number of categories − 1) and a significance level, usually p = 0.05. If χ² is <b>less</b> than the critical value, you <b>fail to reject</b> the null hypothesis: the data are consistent with the expected ratio and chance can explain the difference. If χ² is <b>greater</b>, you reject it: the difference is probably not just chance.'],
 ('observed counts that differed from the prediction looked like a failed prediction','that sampling error alone makes counts deviate','a decision rule for "close enough"','chi-square, degrees of freedom and the critical value'),
 [('χ² = Σ (o − e)² / e','sum over every category'),('df = categories − 1','for a ratio test'),('critical value (p = 0.05): df 1: 3.84, df 2: 5.99, df 3: 7.81','from the table'),('χ² < critical → fail to reject the null','difference can be due to chance')],
 [lab('cs-lab','Does this cross fit 3 : 1?',canvas='cs-canvas',aria='Bars comparing observed and expected counts for a 3 to 1 ratio, with the chi-square result.',
   ctl=[('cs-n','Total offspring',40,400,4,200),('cs-o','Observed dominant phenotype',0,400,1,162)],
   out=[('cs-chi','χ²','x'),('cs-df','degrees of freedom','v'),('cs-crit','critical value, p = 0.05','a'),('cs-dec','decision','')],own=True)],
 [('Total 200, observed dominant 162 (expected 150). Is that consistent with 3 : 1? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cs-n",200],["#cs-o",162]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','χ² = 12²/150 + 12²/50 = 0.96 + 2.88 = 3.84, essentially at the critical value of 3.84 (the boundary of the decision). Try 160 to see a clear fail-to-reject.'),
  ('Keep the ratio but double the total to 400 with 324 dominant. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cs-n",400],["#cs-o",324]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The same percentage miss now gives χ² = 7.68, well past 3.84. Bigger samples make the same proportional deviation more convincing.')],
 ('A cross is expected to give 9 : 3 : 3 : 1. Among 160 offspring you count 94, 28, 26, 12. Test the fit.',[
  '<b>Expected counts.</b> 160 × 9/16 = 90, 160 × 3/16 = 30, 30, and 160/16 = 10.',
  '<b>Each term (o − e)²/e.</b> 16/90 = 0.18; 4/30 = 0.13; 16/30 = 0.53; 4/10 = 0.40.',
  '<b>Add.</b> χ² ≈ 1.24 with df = 4 − 1 = 3.',
  '<b>Decide.</b> The critical value for df 3 at p = 0.05 is 7.81. Since 1.24 &lt; 7.81 you fail to reject the null: the data fit 9:3:3:1.']),
 ['Using percentages or proportions instead of counts in the formula.','Computing expected from the observed totals incorrectly (expected must come from the ratio and the same total).','Using df = number of categories instead of categories − 1.','Saying "accept the null". You can only fail to reject it.','Thinking a small χ² proves the hypothesis. It means chance is a sufficient explanation.'],
 ('Writing the conclusion',['State the null hypothesis ("the observed ratio fits 3:1, deviations are due to chance"), give χ² and df, compare with the critical value, and conclude. "χ² = 3.1 &lt; 3.84, so I fail to reject the null; the data are consistent with 3:1."']),
 [('For a cross with 4 phenotype classes, the degrees of freedom are',['3','4','1','2'],0,'df = number of categories − 1 = 3.')])

mod('expdesign','Experimental design',
 'Every experiment is a fair test of one idea. If two things change at once, you cannot say which one mattered.',
 ['The <b>independent variable</b> is the one thing you change on purpose. The <b>dependent variable</b> is what you measure in response. A good test also has a <b>control group</b> that does not get the treatment (so you have something to compare with) and <b>constants</b>: everything else held the same, such as temperature, volume, time and source of organisms.',
  'Two more ideas make conclusions trustworthy. <b>Replicates</b> (several samples per group) let you see natural variation and compute error bars. A <b>hypothesis</b> is a testable prediction, often written "If ... then ... because ...". A conclusion must stay inside the data: say what changed, by how much, and what that supports, without claiming more than the design can show.'],
 ('an experiment was just doing a thing and seeing what happened','that control, constants and replicates are what turn a result into evidence','why one trial is not enough','independent, dependent and control variables and replicates'),
 [('independent variable','what you change'),('dependent variable','what you measure'),('control group','no treatment, baseline for comparison'),('constants','everything else held equal')],
 [lab('ed-lab','Pick an experiment and name its parts',canvas='ed-canvas',aria='Diagram of a control group and treatment groups with the variables labelled.',
   seg=('ed-sc','Scenario',[('pl','fertilizer and plants'),('en','temperature and catalase'),('ye','sugar and yeast')]),
   ctl=[('ed-n','Replicates per group',1,10,1,3)],
   out=[('ed-iv','independent variable','x'),('ed-dv','dependent variable','a'),('ed-ct','control','v'),('ed-co','held constant',''),('ed-rep','samples in total','')],own=True)],
 [('Choose the temperature and catalase scenario. Which group is the control? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#ed-sc [data-k=en]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Catalase at room temperature (about 22 °C) with the same hydrogen peroxide volume and time. It is the baseline the heated and chilled groups are compared with.'),
  ('Set replicates to 1, then to 6. What changes? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ed-n",1]) + '\'>Set 1</button>','','Total samples go from 3 to 18 here. With one sample per group you cannot tell whether a difference is real or ordinary variation; with six you can compute a mean and SEM.')],
 ('Design a test of whether light color affects the rate of oxygen production by an aquatic plant. Name the variables and a control.',[
  '<b>Independent variable.</b> Color (wavelength) of light: for example red, green, blue, and white.',
  '<b>Dependent variable.</b> Rate of oxygen produced, such as bubbles per minute or mL of gas collected per 10 minutes.',
  '<b>Control group.</b> Plants under white light (or a plant in the dark as a negative control).',
  '<b>Constants.</b> Plant species and size, light intensity, distance from the lamp, temperature, water volume and bicarbonate concentration, and time. <b>Replicates.</b> Several plants per color.']),
 ['Changing more than one variable at a time.','Calling the thing you measure the independent variable.','Having no control group, so there is nothing to compare with.','Using one trial and calling the result reliable.','Writing a conclusion that claims more than the data show (for example proving a mechanism the experiment did not test).'],
 ('Writing FRQ answers about design',['Use the words the question uses. "Identify" means name it. "Describe" means state what you would do or see. A strong design answer names the independent variable, the dependent variable you would measure and how, a control, at least two constants, and replicates. A prediction needs a reason: "If more fertilizer is added, growth will increase up to a point, because nitrogen limits protein synthesis."']),
 [('Which is the best control for a test of whether a drug lowers blood pressure in mice?',['a group of mice given an inactive substance in the same way','a group given double the drug','mice of a different species','no mice, only a graph'],0,'The control matches everything except the drug, so any difference can be attributed to it.')])

mod('ratesq10','Rates, Q10 & dilutions',
 'Biology is full of "how fast": how fast a reaction runs, how fast a population grows, how diluted a solution is. A few formulas cover most of it.',
 ['A <b>rate</b> is a change per unit time: rate = Δquantity / Δtime. On a graph of quantity against time, the rate is the slope. To compare conditions, measure the same change over the same time.',
  'The <b>temperature coefficient Q<sub>10</sub></b> says how much a rate changes for a 10 °C rise: Q<sub>10</sub> = (R<sub>2</sub>/R<sub>1</sub>)<sup>10/(T<sub>2</sub> − T<sub>1</sub>)</sup>. Many biological rates have Q<sub>10</sub> near 2. This only holds below the temperature that denatures the enzymes. A <b>serial dilution</b> makes a solution weaker in equal steps: each step multiplies the dilution by the same factor.'],
 ('a rate was just "how much happened"','the time over which the change was measured','rates depend strongly on temperature','rate, Q10 and dilution calculations'),
 [('rate = Δquantity / Δtime','slope of the graph'),('Q<sub>10</sub> = (R<sub>2</sub>/R<sub>1</sub>)<sup>10/(T<sub>2</sub>−T<sub>1</sub>)</sup>','temperature coefficient'),('C<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> = C<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>','dilution of a stock'),('after k steps: C = C<sub>0</sub> / factor<sup>k</sup>','serial dilution')],
 [lab('rq-lab','Rate against temperature',canvas='rq-canvas',aria='Graph of reaction rate against temperature for the chosen Q10.',
   ctl=[('rq-t','Temperature',5,40,1,25),('rq-q','Q10',1,4,.1,2)],
   out=[('rq-r','rate (relative to 20 °C = 10)','x'),('rq-r10','rate 10 °C colder','a'),('rq-rat','ratio of the two','v')],own=True),
  lab('rq-lab2','Serial dilution',canvas='rq-canvas2',aria='A row of test tubes, each a tenfold or other fixed dilution of the one before.',
   seg=('rq-f','Dilution factor per step',[('2','1 : 2'),('5','1 : 5'),('10','1 : 10')]),
   ctl=[('rq-c0','Starting concentration (mg/mL)',1,100,1,80)],
   out=[('rq-c3','tube 3','x'),('rq-c5','tube 5','a'),('rq-tot','total dilution at tube 5','v')],own=True)],
 [('Set Q10 to 2 and the temperature to 30 °C. By how much did the rate change from 20 °C? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#rq-t",30],["#rq-q",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','It doubled (10 → 20). That is what Q10 = 2 means for a 10 °C rise.'),
  ('Make a 1 : 10 dilution series starting at 80 mg/mL. What is tube 3? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#rq-f [data-k=\"10\"]"],["#rq-c0",80]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','80 / 10³ = 0.08 mg/mL. Every tube is a tenth of the one before.')],
 ('A reaction runs at 6 µmol/min at 20 °C and 15 µmol/min at 35 °C. Find Q10 and estimate the rate at 25 °C.',[
  '<b>Setup.</b> R1 = 6 at 20 °C, R2 = 15 at 35 °C, so T2 − T1 = 15.',
  '<b>Q10.</b> (15/6)<sup>10/15</sup> = 2.5<sup>0.667</sup> ≈ 1.84.',
  '<b>Rate at 25 °C.</b> That is 5 °C above 20, so multiply by 1.84<sup>5/10</sup> = 1.36: 6 × 1.36 ≈ 8.1 µmol/min.']),
 ['Using the temperature difference in the exponent as 1 instead of 10/ΔT.','Forgetting to square-root or cube-root fractional exponents when ΔT is not 10.','Applying Q10 above the temperature where enzymes denature.','In serial dilutions, using the factor once instead of once per tube.','Comparing rates measured over different time intervals.'],
 ('Reading rates from graphs',['For a straight section of a quantity-time graph, rate is rise over run with units. If the graph curves, take the slope of the tangent at the point asked (an initial rate is the slope near t = 0). Always keep the units, for example mL O<sub>2</sub> per minute.']),
 [('A rate doubles when temperature rises by 10 °C. Q10 is',['2','10','1','20'],0,'Q10 is the factor by which the rate changes per 10 °C.')])
